<h2 id="who">1. Who is responsible for your data</h2>

<p>
  The data controller for this website is <strong>Stefanos Tzortzoglou</strong>, a private individual based in
  Uppsala, Sweden. This site is a personal portfolio, not a business: nothing is sold here and no company is behind
  it.
</p>
<p>
  Contact for any privacy question or request:
  <a href="mailto:stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu">stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Because the controller is established in Sweden, this policy is governed by the EU General Data Protection Regulation
  (GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679) and the Swedish Data Protection Act
  (<span lang="sv">lag (2018:218) med kompletterande bestämmelser till EU:s dataskyddsförordning</span>).
</p>

<h2 id="browsing">2. If you only browse the site</h2>

<p>You do not need an account to read anything on the public pages. You are asked for information only if you
  choose to send it: by creating an account (<a href="#account">section 3</a>) or by using the contact form
  (<a href="#contact">section 4</a>).
  Three things still happen automatically, because that is how the web works:</p>

<h3 id="hosting-logs">2.1 Hosting logs</h3>
<p>
  The site is hosted on <strong>GitHub Pages</strong> (GitHub, Inc., United States). To deliver and protect the pages,
  GitHub processes technical connection data including your <strong>IP address</strong>, the requested URL, and your
  browser's user-agent string. I have no access to these logs and cannot read them.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> serving the site and protecting it from abuse.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — running a functioning, secure website.</li>
  <li><strong>Transfer outside the EEA:</strong> yes, to the United States, under GitHub's Data Protection Agreement and
    the EU Standard Contractual Clauses. See
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="analytics">2.2 Analytics</h3>
<p>
  If you accept the banner, page views are counted by a small endpoint on <strong>api.tzortzoglou.eu</strong> — the
  same application that receives the contact form, running on a virtual private server rented in my own name. No
  analytics company is involved and nothing is loaded from one: your browser sends one short message to my own server,
  which sends nothing back.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What a counted page view records:</strong> the path of the page — without its query string or fragment,
    so anything sitting in this site's own address bar stays in your browser — which of my two sites it was, the
    <em>host</em> of the page that linked you here — <code>www.linkedin.com</code>, not the full address — and the
    time.</li>
  <li><strong>What it does not record:</strong> no cookie, no IP address, no device or browser fingerprint, no
    identifier of any kind, and never the full referring URL. That last one is worth being exact about, because it is
    the one place a search term can ride: your browser hands the referring address over in full, as it does to every
    site you click through to, and my server reduces it to its host and discards the rest before anything is written.
    The trimming is real, but it happens on arrival rather than before sending. Nothing ties one page view to another,
    so there is no profile and nothing to build one out of.</li>
  <li><strong>Where the request goes:</strong> to that server, which I run — so unlike the hosting logs in
    <a href="#hosting-logs">section 2.1</a>, this one connection is to me rather than to GitHub. Your IP address arrives
    with it, because every request carries one, and it is used there for a single purpose: as the key a rate limiter
    counts against, held in memory, so that the endpoint cannot be hammered. It is never written to the page view, never
    stored, and never looked at.</li>
  <li><strong>How long a counted page view is kept:</strong> there is no scheduled deletion today, and that is a
    decision rather than an oversight. A row identifies nobody and cannot be traced back to a person, so there is
    nothing in it to expire on anyone's behalf, and the table is small enough that a deletion job would be machinery
    built for a problem this site does not have. If it ever grows to the point of mattering, rows older than 24 months
    will be deleted and this paragraph will say so.</li>
  <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> understanding, in aggregate, which pages people find useful.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent, Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR. Nothing is counted at all unless you accept it
    on the banner shown on your first visit, and you can withdraw that consent at any time — as easily as you gave it —
    from the <a href="/cookies/">Cookies &amp; Local Storage</a> page (Art. 7(3)).</li>
  <li><strong>If you decline or ignore the banner:</strong> nothing is sent, nothing is counted, and the site works
    exactly the same.</li>
  <li><strong>Before September 2026</strong> this was done by <strong>GoatCounter</strong>, a third-party service.
    Nothing is sent there any more; the figures it gathered up to the switchover are history rather than a live source,
    and still readable at
    <a href="https://steftzor.goatcounter.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">steftzor.goatcounter.com</a>.
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Third-party files loaded by your browser</h3>
<p>
  Some files are loaded directly from other providers rather than from this domain. When your browser fetches those
  files, it connects to those providers directly, and they necessarily receive your
  <strong>IP address</strong>, your user-agent, and the page you were on. This happens on page load, before you
  interact with anything. On these public pages that is now one provider on one page: the rendered GitHub statistics
  images on the <a href="/code/">Code</a> page. The typeface and every icon are served from this domain. The full list is in the
  <a href="/cookies/">Cookies &amp; Local Storage</a> page.
</p>
<p>
  I do not control what those providers do with that connection data. If you would rather not connect to them, a
  content-blocking browser extension will prevent the requests; the site remains readable, though those statistics
  images will not render. The application behind the sign-in loads more from elsewhere than these
  pages do, including a map, which <a href="#map-tiles">section 3.3</a> is about.
</p>

<h2 id="account">3. If you create an account</h2>

<p>
  Some personal content is shared with specific people through a separate application at
  <a href="https://app.tzortzoglou.eu/">app.tzortzoglou.eu</a>, where signing in, registering and the private area all
  live. Reaching it requires an account, which is approved manually by me, and each account carries a role that decides
  what it may see. Creating an account is entirely optional — nothing on this public site depends on it.
</p>

