    that address in one step, and reports back exactly what it removed.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  <strong>What is never done with it:</strong> your email address is not used for marketing, not added to any mailing
  list, not sold, not shared with anyone, and not used for profiling or automated decision-making within the meaning of
  Art. 22 GDPR.
</p>

<h3 id="app-analytics">3.2 Page loads inside the application</h3>
<p>
  The signed-in application counts its own page loads, and it does so for everyone who uses it, with no banner and no
  question. A page load is counted only after the server has confirmed the session behind it: if you are not signed in,
  nothing is counted and you are sent to the sign-in form instead. That ordering is what the paragraph below rests on —
  every page load counted here belongs to an approved account holder, or it is not counted.
  To be exact about what that does and does not promise: it is a statement about the app's own
  pages, which is where the counts come from. The endpoint that receives them is open, as a page
  counter has to be, so a request made by hand rather than by a browser could add a row that no
  session stands behind. That is why these counts are treated as a measure of traffic and never as
  a record of who did what — there is nothing in a row to tie it to a person in the first place.
  It records exactly the four things listed in <a href="#analytics">section 2.2</a> — the path, that it was
  the application rather than the public site, the referring host, and the time — and the same nothing else: no cookie,
  no IP address, no identifier. In particular it does not record <em>which account</em> was signed in. A counted page
  load is not linked to you, and cannot be linked back to you afterwards.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Why you are not asked:</strong> the public site's counter measures an audience of strangers, which is
    something you are entitled to refuse. The application is a service you deliberately signed in to, and counting how
    often its own pages are opened is part of operating it — knowing what works and what is unused — rather than
    measuring an audience. Asking a question I would have to keep working regardless would be the theatre this site
    avoids elsewhere.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — operating, maintaining and improving a
    service you are signed in to. The ePrivacy consent rule in Art. 5(3) is not engaged, because nothing is stored on
    or read from your device to do this.</li>
  <li><strong>Object to it:</strong> you can, under Art. 21 (see <a href="#rights">section 6</a>). Since the record
    holds nothing that identifies you, the honest remedy is to say so and I will stop counting the application
    entirely rather than pretend to filter you out of it.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="map-tiles">3.3 Map tiles inside the application</h3>
<p>
  Two pages in the application draw a map: the race location on <strong>/f1/</strong> and the stops on
  <strong>/transit/</strong>. The map software is served from my own domain, but the map picture is not. Your browser
  fetches it a square at a time, as you pan and zoom, straight from <strong>tiles.openfreemap.org</strong> —
  <strong>OpenFreeMap</strong>, a free map-tile service built on OpenStreetMap data. It asks for no account, no key
  and no payment, which is the only reason your browser can be the one asking rather than my server.
  Your browser already fetches a couple of other files from outside this domain — the sign-in libraries, and on one
  page an animation — and those are listed on the <a href="/cookies/">Cookies &amp; Local Storage</a> page. Tiles are
  worth their own section because they are not a file. The same script is fetched once and is the same for everybody;
  which tiles you ask for depends on what you are looking at, and you ask for more of them the longer you look.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What they receive:</strong> your <strong>IP address</strong>, your user-agent, and which tiles you asked
    for. Those tile numbers are themselves a location — not where you are, but which part of the world the map is
    showing you and how closely you are looking at it. Nothing about your account goes with the request: it carries no
    token, no name and no email address, because it is an ordinary image request and there is nowhere in one to put
    them.</li>
  <li><strong>When:</strong> only on those two pages, only once a map is actually drawn there, and only for someone
    signed in — no page on the public site contacts them at all.</li>
  <li><strong>Why it is not proxied like everything else:</strong> every outside service this application takes data
    from — the weather, the departures, the Formula 1 results — is called by my server, so your browser never meets
    it. Map tiles are the exception because they are images, a few
    hundred of them in a session of panning around, and putting my server in front of those would mean running a tile
    service rather than passing a message along. I chose a map over that, and this paragraph is the price of choosing
    it.</li>
  <li><strong>A request I have no record of:</strong> it goes from your browser to them and never passes through
    anything I run, so I have no log of it, no count of it, and no way to tell you what they did with it afterwards.
    What I can tell you is exactly what the code asks for, which is above, and that they are not my processor: there is
    no contract between us, so whatever they receive they hold as their own controller, under their own terms and
    wherever their network serves you from, which may be outside the EEA.</li>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — the same basis as the rest of the
    application in <a href="#app-analytics">section 3.2</a>: showing a departure board or a race weekend on a map is
    part of the service you signed in to use. Your right to object under Art. 21 applies here too (see
    <a href="#rights">section 6</a>).</li>
  <li><strong>If you would rather not:</strong> a content-blocking extension, or a browser in strict tracking-protection
    mode, will stop the requests, and both pages carry on working — the departures and the race details come from my
    own server and do not depend on the map. A map that cannot load says so instead of showing you an empty rectangle.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="contact">4. If you contact me</h2>

<h3 id="contact-email">4.1 If you email me directly</h3>
<p>
  Email sent to <a href="mailto:stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu">stefanos@tzortzoglou.eu</a> is not delivered to a mailbox on
  this domain. It is relayed by my domain registrar's mail-forwarding service
  (<strong>Spaceship</strong>) to a personal <strong>Gmail</strong> mailbox (Google Ireland Limited), where it is read
  and answered. Both providers process the message in transit or at rest as part of delivering it.
</p>
<p>
  Its contents, your address, and anything you choose to include are kept for as long as needed to deal with your
  message and to keep a record of the correspondence.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Legal basis:</strong> legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — responding to someone who contacted me.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Transfer outside the EEA:</strong> possible, under those providers' own standard contractual clauses.</li>
  <li><strong>Sensitive information:</strong> ordinary email is not end-to-end encrypted. Please do not send anything
    confidential or sensitive to this address.</li>
</ul>

